WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.550
The coronavirus pandemic has remapped everyone’s worlds.

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:07.120
How has that looked for you?

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:12.130
CityLab is an online news magazine about urban
life and places that matter.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:15.160
In April, we asked readers to share homemade
maps

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:18.599
depicting how the pandemic has changed their lives.

00:00:18.599 --> 00:00:23.140
Within a month, we’d received more than
300 submissions from around the world.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:28.560
Using pencil, watercolors, clay, found objects
and many other media, readers mapped a rich

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.750
spectrum of perspectives, experiences, hopes,
and frustrations - from the places they fled,

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:39.610
to the people they miss, to the nature, neighbors,
and secret spots of solace they cling to.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:42.700
Some showed the ways their home lives had been hemmed in

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.820
and rearranged by the virus outside.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:49.670
Many readers infused their maps with humor,
like this one sheltering in her apartment

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.880
in New Delhi.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.800
And this one, from California, of a relationship
tested by extreme mundanity.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:09.119
With distractions in scarce supply, several
readers turned to fantasy for inspiration.

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:13.789
From Spain to Vietnam to Canada, neighborhoods
were perceived in new ways.

00:01:13.789 --> 00:01:20.009
“The lockdown has made people scatter from
the streets, and with them cars and noise,”

00:01:20.009 --> 00:01:23.280
wrote one reader about her map of silence
in surrounding Bilbao.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.140
“I found myself listening more intently
as I walked and forming new dictionaries of

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:33.920
sound in my head,” wrote another person
about the sounds of Cambridge, Massachusetts.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.627
A routine walk focused the attention of this

00:01:36.627 --> 00:01:38.780
reader in Austin, Texas, who wrote:

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:44.180
"I've become cognizant of the tiny details in people's yards that reveal things about their lives.”

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:49.060
But for a lot of people, the contrast between
inside and outside felt really dark.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.040
Many maps were laced with stress and anxiety.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:56.219
Lonely landscapes were marked with memories
of social contact.

00:01:56.219 --> 00:02:01.039
With school closures and travel restrictions
upending their lives, several readers wished

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.600
they could be somewhere other than where they
were, like this college student

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.620
unhappy to be back at home.

00:02:07.639 --> 00:02:11.460
And this Italian expatriate in the U.S., missing
her family in Bergamo.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:17.210
“Even if the pandemic has made physical
distances more painful, in my heart, my beloved

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:19.450
worlds are even closer.”

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:23.550
Some envisioned a better world, like a Miami
resident who thinks more public transit could

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:27.200
reconnect the city once lockdowns lift.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.810
Access to nature seemed to make all the difference.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:35.340
In Sydney, Australia, a reader found herself
looking closer at the trees on her block.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:40.290
In the U.K., a writer sketched what she called
“a beautiful old canyon near our house that

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.530
looks like a lost world.”

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:47.080
Though each submission was unique, put together
these maps show how the emotional experiences

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:51.760
of the coronavirus pandemic have been shared
by people all over the world.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:58.840
As economies reopen and a wave of protests
fill formerly empty streets, public and private

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.900
spaces are shifting once again, as societies
struggle to balance the sweeping impacts of

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.870
the pandemic and a massive call to address
systemic racism and injustice.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:15.380
As a powerful medium for revealing our relationships
to place, these maps serve as a reminder of

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:19.560
the ways we are still connected - especially
through times of upheaval,

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:22.920
and as new futures are forged.

